1.  An Introduction
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
  This program was written after seeing the Finger option from the Waffle
BBS software for the PC.  Falcon can provide some information, but it
doesn't really tell the user anything interesting.

  As of version 2.03, UserInfo only works with Kickstart V37+, and as of
version 2.20 may be used from Workbench as long as ReqTools V38+ is
available.



2.  Usage
~~~~~~~~~
  UserInfo can mainly be used from the CLI.  It requires only one argument
which is the partial (or full) name of the user that you which to examine.

  If run from workbench, UserInfo will try to open reqtools.library, V38+,
and make use of it.  If Reqtools isn't available, UserInfo can only be
used from the Shell/CLI.


  This utility would generally be used from within Falcon, so I have
included an example Menus.BBS entry.


R CON -- "UserInfo \"«GETSTACK»«Name (or part name) of user to view? »\""


  This will either accept a command stack with the user name on it, or it
will bring up a line asking the user for a name.  You may use any key that
you like to run UserInfo, but seeing as the way Brian has done the menus,
it makes it a little hard to find a useful one.

  The reason that the quotes are needed is that if someone places a +
character at the end of the name to check, it nicely drops the user into a
shell.  It's not my fault, it has to do with the parsing in the shell.


  The name search is case insensitive, and allows partial names.  You will
be asked each time it finds a possible match.  If the name is not the one
wanted, just enter N at the prompt, and UserInfo will search for another
match.  If none are found then UserInfo will just exit out informing the
user that a match could not be found.


  Two entries will also be made into your Log.BBS file if you are using
one.  The first is the text that is being scanned for, and if a successful
match was found, the name of the user being scanned is entered into the
log.  This is now optional.  See the config info for more details.


  As of Version 2.20, I have included a kind of Graphic Interface via the
usage of ReqTools requestors.  This means that you don't need a console
window open somewhere.  This is what is used when run from Workbench.

  This can be accessed via the CLI be placing the argument -r instead
of the username to search for.


There are three (four if using SysOp mode) requestors.

  The first is a simple string gagdet into which you type the name (or
part name) of the user you want to scan for.

  The second requestor only appears if you have placed an ampersand in the
search string.  If the approprate setting is selected in the config file,
what ever you type into the string will be invisable.

  The third requestor is the check requestor.  This simply comes up asking
if the possible match UserInfo has found is the one that you wish to view.

  The fourth requestor is simply the Info screen.  Please note that this
generlly screws up the positioning when using a proportinal font :(



2a. Config file
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
  As of V2.11 the format for the config file has changed (as well as it's
name).  Older config files are not compatible.

  The config is called UserInfo.prefs and should be placed in either the
directory that you are calling UserInfo from, or in the ENV:  directory.

  The file is standard ASCII with one option per line of the file.  The
available options are:


USERFILE=<path/filename>
  The file name and path of the User.BBS file to get the information from.
If this option is not included, then UserInfo is not usable.  I no longer
try to track down the User.BBS file.  This should mean that it will work
with all configurations.


APPFILE=<path/filename>
  This is the filename and path of the optional APP.BBS file.  If you are
using this option in the BBS, then as much information as you want will be
extracted from this file for the user to be scanned.

  If you are not using this, or an entry cannot be found, then this extra
data will not be displayed.


COMMENTFILE=<path/filename>
  This is a optional extra information filename/path.  See section 2b for
more information about using this.


DISPLAY=xxxx  <four switches>
  This option allows you to select what extra (if any) information you
want to be extracted from the APPFile.BBS.

  The format for this entry is four letters.  They are basicly switches,
with Y being on and N for off.  The defualts for all are N (off).  The
four switches relate to the following information (in this order):
                Address, Phone number, Age, Computer Type

  The most common setting you would probably use would be to hide all of
the information other than the computer type.  This would mean that your
config file would have this in it:  NNNY


PASSWORD=<password string>
  This is for the SysOp's use only.  The sysop of each system can choose
to view all of the available data about a user no matter what the setting
in the DISPLAY option is.  This is only useful if you have the APPFILE
option set, but I may expand this to include extra information other than
that shown (depending upon demand)

  To access this extra information, simply enter an ampersand (@) as the
first letter of the string that you want to search for.  This will be
removed from the search string, and the password will be asked of you.

  The password is not case sensitive, and should be something that no-one
else can guess.


HIDEPASSWORD=<Yes/No>
  When used in conjunction with the above function, passwords entered when
running UserInfo will not be visable.  This means that if someone is
looking over your shoulder they will not know what your password is.  The
defualt is ON.


NOLOG=<Yes/No>
  If this is set to Yes (defualt) then UserInfo will try to make two
entries into your Log.BBS file.  The first will be the name that was
entered to scan for, and the second will be the user viewed (if one is
selected).



2b. Comments
~~~~~~~~~~~~
  As of 2.05, I have added the option for comments to be placed after the
name of the user being scanned.  This was just to provide a little more
information (say, the users real name if they are using an alias).

  The file is a standard ASCII text file, which will be loaded from the
entry provided in the configuration file.

  There should be one line for each user that you want to have a comment
for.  The line should look like you want it to appear when it is shown on
screen.  The users name must be in full at the start of the line, but
after that, you can have whatever you want.


An example (taken from my system) is:

David Dustin (The BOSS!)
Renze Deruiter (The Renze)
Polly Ester (Here kitty)


  You do not have to have an entry for each user if you don't want.  If an
entry cannot be found for the user being scanned, then nothing extra will
be added :)




3.  History
~~~~~~~~~~~
V1.00	10 August 1992
    * First release.

V1.01   12 August 1992
    * Corrected a few errors
    * Cleaned up output
    * Added "Total Time OnLine"

V1.10   15 August 1992
    * Removed need for the full database to be loaded.  Now scans through
      the file to find the user's information
    * Cleaned up display/handling
    * Added date of last call (finally worked out how Brian stored the date)

V2.00   Never released
    * Changed code a bit, and started to make use of my library

V2.01   20 September
    * Rewrote more code, and made full use of ECLIPSE.Library (mine)

V2.02   21 September
    * Changed search method.  You can now specify ANY part of the name
      (even one letter), and it will scan properly

V2.02b  23 September
    * Changed method of checking for a keypress.  Now uses SetMode() to
      change the console to a RAW window
    * Now requires V37+ dos.library

V2.03   7 November
    * Added extra information from APPFILE data file (Thanks Dave :)
    * Included a config to specify what data from the APPFILE is to be
      shown
    * Removed a couple of little annoying bugs

V2.04   9 November
    * Cleaned up code again
    * Added a SysOp override of the config file.  If you put a '@' as the
      first letter of the name to scan, it will provide all the information
      that it can about the user (as long as there is an entry for them in
      the APPFILE)

V2.05   10 Novemeber
    * Added comment feild and file to information.  Allows you to provide an
      insight into what you think of the user :)
    * Released seperatly away from my ECLIPSE.library beta test archive.

V2.06   10 November (after I woke up again ;)
    * Corrected an error that had popped up with my shuffling round of the
      code.  The text that was being scanned for wasn't being entered into
      the logfile

V2.07   10 November (even later in the day)
    * There was a bug if all of the APP options were not selected.  It would
      not read the correct line from the file. (Thanks Karl)

V2.08   10 November (Damn.  Too many bugs in one day)
    * The SysOp mode wasn't working unless you ran it from the shell.

V2.09   27 November
    * Put in a password system on the SysOp info mode, to prevent
      'illegal' usage of the funtion
    * Added extra paths for comments file

V2.09b  28 November
    * Made same mistake as I had made with V2.05 (That'll teach me to update
      and/or improve my code :)

V2.10   15 December
    * Re-wrote config file routine and format to make it easier to upgrade
      and update.  Also added more options to the config, like the
      paths/name of the various files, so that I don't have to try and
      guess where the files are (it's all your fault Dave H.  :)

V2.11   16 December
    * Re-wrote text output routines.  Now uses RawDoFmt() so as to make it
      easier to upgrade (I'm getting lazy)

V2.20   18 December
    * Added "GUI" using ReqTools V38
    * Now usable via Workbench (using ReqTools)
    * Password entry now optionaly invisable
    * Log output optional
    * Cleaned up code, and removed "possibly" fatal bug

V2.21   19 December
    * Finished major modifications and bug testing of V2.20
    * Added backspace to Hidden password entry from console (should work
      with all systems)

V2.21b  19 December
    * Fixed small error with single-key press read routine
    * Optomized slightly (lost 20 bytes :)
    * Fixed bug with error checking routines which caused ReqTools not
      to be accessed properly.  This ment no requestors were being used.

V2.22   24 December
    * Corrected error with lengths of infomation


28-Jan-1996
    * Released into public domain by the author Dave Dustin



